,"g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":36.420583,"lo":25.431294,"d":309},{"n":"Laundry","g":"essentials","k":"laundry","la":36.420418,"lo":25.431915,"d":318},{"n":"Pharmacy","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":36.416051,"lo":25.432923,"d":339},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":36.420647,"lo":25.432021,"d":345,"h":"24/7"},{"n":"Φηρά Bus Terminal","g":"essentials","k":"bus_station","la":36.41655,"lo":25.433429,"d":346,"wifi":1},{"n":"Vourvoulos","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.435,"lo":25.436667,"d":1982,"ds":"Vourvoulos (Greek: Βουρβούλος) is a village on the island of Santorini in Greece. It is located 3,7 kilometers northeast of the capital Fira, built on a slope facing the east shore of the island. Vourvoulos is part of the Thira region and had 591 permanent inhabitants according to the Greek census of 2021.","wpu":"Vourvoulos","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Vourvoulos.jpg/960px-Vourvoulos.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Imerovigli","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.433333,"lo":25.416667,"d":2080,"ds":"Imerovigli (Greek: Ημεροβίγλι) is a village on the island of Santorini, Greece, situated north of the island capital Fira. Imerovigli is built on the rim of the Santorini caldera and is crossed by narrow, paved paths. Imerovigli's churches are all made in the Cycladic architectural style.","wpu":"Imerovigli","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/da/Imerovigli_01.jpg/960px-Imerovigli_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Minoan eruption","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.41,"lo":25.4,"d":2828,"ds":"The Minoan eruption was a catastrophic volcanic eruption that devastated the Aegean island of Thera (also called Santorini) circa 1600 BC. It destroyed the Minoan settlement at Akrotiri, as well as communities and agricultural areas on nearby islands and the coast of Crete with subsequent earthquakes and tsunamis.","wpu":"Minoan eruption","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Santorini_ASTER.jpg/960px-Santorini_ASTER.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Nea Kameni","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.4,"lo":25.4,"d":3348,"ds":"Nea Kameni is a small, uninhabited Greek island of volcanic origin located in the Aegean Sea, within the flooded Santorini caldera. Nea Kameni (new burnt) and the neighbouring small island Palea Kameni (old burnt) have formed over the past two millennia through repeated eruptions of dacite lava and ash.","wpu":"Nea Kameni","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/06/Santorini_ASTER.jpg/960px-Santorini_ASTER.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Pyrgos Kallistis","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.383056,"lo":25.449444,"d":4257,"ds":"Pyrgos Kallistis (Greek: Πύργος Καλλίστης, lit. 'Beauty Tower') or simply Pyrgos (Greek: Πύργος, lit. 'Tower') is a village on the Aegean island of Santorini, Greece, in the Cyclades archipelago with a population of 1,078 according to the 2021 census. Pyrgos is part of the Municipality of Thira and is situated approximately 7 km away from the island's capital Fira.","wpu":"Pyrgos Kallistis","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/13/Pyrgos-Santorini.jpg/960px-Pyrgos-Santorini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Episkopi Gonias","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.385,"lo":25.459722,"d":4532,"ds":"Episkopi Gonias (Greek: Επισκοπή Γωνιάς) also Mesa Gonia (Greek: Μέσα Γωνιά) is a village and a community on the island of Santorini in Greece, located 6 km southeast of the capital Fira. The village is built on the foothills of Profitis Ilias mountain and had 187 inhabitants according to the 2021 census. Episkopi Gonias was almost entirely destroyed by an earthquake in 1956.","wpu":"Episkopi Gonias","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Episkopi_Gonias.jpg/960px-Episkopi_Gonias.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Palea Kameni","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.3975,"lo":25.379444,"d":5066,"ds":"Palea Kameni, also known as Palia Kameni, is a volcanic island within the Santorini Caldera. The island was formed by a series of volcanic eruptions that formed large deposits of pumice and dacite lavas. The island's name translates to \"Old Burnt Island\".","wpu":"Palea Kameni","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/95/Palaia_Kameni_south_side.jpg/960px-Palaia_Kameni_south_side.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Panagia Episkopi","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.3775,"lo":25.464167,"d":5443,"ds":"The Panagia Episkopi (Greek: Παναγία Επισκοπή) is a middle-Byzantine Greek Orthodox former cathedral, now church, on the island of Santorini (Thira), in the Cyclades region of Greece. It is also called Panagia tis Episkopis (Παναγία της Επισκοπής) or Church of Episkopi Thiras (Ναός Επισκοπής Θήρας).","wpu":"Panagia Episkopi","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Episkopi_Gonias_%282798407068%29.jpg/960px-Episkopi_Gonias_%282798407068%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kamari","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.376775,"lo":25.481828,"d":6521,"ds":"Kamari (Greek: Καμάρι) is a coastal village on the southeastern part of the Aegean island of Santorini, Greece, in the Cyclades archipelago with a population of 1,065 according to the 2021 census. It is part of the Municipality of Thira and is situated approximately 8 km away from the island's capital Fira.","wpu":"Kamari","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/07/Santorini_kamari.jpg/960px-Santorini_kamari.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Emporeio, Santorini","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.358333,"lo":25.444444,"d":6759,"ds":"Emporeio (Greek: Εμπορείο), also known as Nimborios (Greek: Νημποριός), is a village on the island of Santorini, Greece. It is located 12 km from Fira, near the Profitis Ilias Mountains. According to the 2021 census, the community of Emporio has 3,704 permanent inhabitants.","wpu":"Emporeio, Santorini","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Emporeio001.jpg/960px-Emporeio001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sellada","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.3684,"lo":25.4741,"d":6782,"ds":"Sellada (Greek: Σελλάδα) is a mountain pass between the peaks of Profitis Ilias and Mesa Vouno, near the village of Kamari in the island of Santorini, Greece. The north-eastern and north-western slopes of Sellada accommodated the necropolis (i.e., cemetery) of Ancient Thera, an ancient city of Santorini.","wpu":"Sellada","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ef/Sellada_NE_sea_view.jpg/960px-Sellada_NE_sea_view.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Amoudi Bay","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.46,"lo":25.370556,"d":7077,"ds":"Amoudi Bay is a bay on the Greek Island of Santorini. The feature is set into the high volcanic cliffs of western Santorini below the town of Oia.","wpu":"Amoudi Bay","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Santorini_AmmoudiBay_tango7174.jpg/960px-Santorini_AmmoudiBay_tango7174.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Castle of Akrotiri","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.3582,"lo":25.3972,"d":7268,"ds":"The Castle of Akrotiri, also known as Castle Akrotiri, Goulas or La Ponta, is a former Venetian castle on the Greek island of Santorini. The now-ruined castle lies at the center of the village of Akrotiri.","wpu":"Castle of Akrotiri","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Akrotiri003.jpg/960px-Akrotiri003.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ancient Thera","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.364586,"lo":25.477939,"d":7327,"ds":"Ancient Thera (Greek: Αρχαία Θήρα) is an archaeological site from classical antiquity on the island of Santorini, which sits on the top of a limestone hill called Mesa Vouno. Starting in 1895, Friedrich Hiller von Gaertringen systematically investigated the city until 1904. Later excavations by N.","wpu":"Ancient Thera","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1f/Santorini_ancient_thera_1.jpg/960px-Santorini_ancient_thera_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Akrotiri (prehistoric city)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.351389,"lo":25.403611,"d":7774,"ds":"Akrotiri is a Cycladic Bronze Age settlement site on the volcanic Greek island of Santorini. The name comes from the nearby village of Akrotiri, the name of the site in antiquity is unknown. The settlement was destroyed during the Theran eruption sometime in the 16th century BC, and was buried in volcanic ash, which helped preserve its fine frescoes.","wpu":"Akrotiri (prehistoric city)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Ancient_Akrotiri.jpeg/960px-Ancient_Akrotiri.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Perissa, Santorini","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.356944,"lo":25.473611,"d":7831,"ds":"Perissa (Greek: Περίσσα) is a village on the island of Santorini, Greece. It is located 13 km to the southeast of Fira, near the Messa Vouno rock. According to the 2021 census, Perissa has 725 permanent inhabitants.","wpu":"Perissa, Santorini","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e3/Perissa_1.jpg/960px-Perissa_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Aspronisi","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.383333,"lo":25.347778,"d":8307,"ds":"Aspronisi (Greek: Ασπρονήσι) is an uninhabited island lying within the Santorini caldera. The island was originally formed by the massive Minoan eruption in the 2nd millennium BC, after which it was gradually built up by successive volcanic activity. The name Aspronisi, which means 'White Island' in Greek, is derived from the island being partially composed of white pumice.","wpu":"Aspronisi","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Aspronisi_02.jpg/960px-Aspronisi_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Red Beach (Santorini)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.34732,"lo":25.39459,"d":8474,"ds":"Red Beach is a volcanic sand beach on the Aegean island of Santorini. The beach is famed for its titular red-hued sand, (oxidised iron) and is a noted for being a popular tourist attraction.","wpu":"Red Beach (Santorini)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0e/Red_Beach%2C_Greece_Oct_29%2C_2018_07-12-47.jpeg/960px-Red_Beach%2C_Greece_Oct_29%2C_2018_07-12-47.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Tomato Industrial Museum D. Nomikos","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.339,"lo":25.4327,"d":8787,"ds":"Tomato Industrial Museum \"D. Nomikos\" (Greek: Βιομηχανικό Μουσείο Τομάτας \"Δ. ΝΟΜΙΚΟΣ\") is an industrial museum in Vlychada on the island of Santorini in Greece. It was founded as a tomato paste factory in 1945 but closed in 1981. In 2014, it reopened as a museum to preserve the remaining buildings, objects and stories connected to Santorini's tomato processing past.","wpu":"Tomato Industrial Museum D. Nomikos","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/Tomato_Industrial_Museum_D._Nomikos_-_exterior_2.jpg/960px-Tomato_Industrial_Museum_D._Nomikos_-_exterior_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Therasia","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.43,"lo":25.33,"d":9046,"ds":"Therasia, also known as Thirasía (Greek: Θηρασία), is an island in the volcanic island group of Santorini in the Greek Cyclades. It lies north-west of Nea Kameni, a small island formed in recent centuries by volcanic activity and thus marking the centre of the island group. Therasia is the second largest island of the group, the largest by far being Thera.","wpu":"Therasia","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/9e/Thirassia.JPG/960px-Thirassia.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Akrotiri Lighthouse","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":36.35779,"lo":25.35706,"d":9351,"ds":"Akrotiri Lighthouse is a 19th-century lighthouse on the Greek island of Santorini. The lighthouse was built by a French company in 1892, making the lighthouse one of the oldest in Greece. The lighthouse ceased operating during World War II. The Greek Navy recommissioned the lighthouse in 1945.","wpu":"Akrotiri Lighthouse","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/FarosSantorini.jpg/960px-FarosSantorini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"wk":"2026-10-18","about":{"ds":"Santorini, officially Thira or Thera, is a Greek island in the southern Aegean Sea, about 200 km (120 mi) southeast from the mainland. It is the largest island of a small, circular archipelago formed by the Santorini caldera. It is the southernmost member of the Cyclades group of islands, with an area of approximately 73 km2 (28 mi2) and a 2021 census population of 15,480.","wpu":"Santorini","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bf/2011_Dimos_Thiras.png/960px-2011_Dimos_Thiras.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}}